WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.820
Today, let's make sense of something you can feel: water pushing on you.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.000
We'll start with pressure, then use it to explain floating.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.820
Pressure tells us how much normal force acts per unit area.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.180
Normal here means perpendicular to the surface, not ordinary.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.800
So pressure equals force divided by area, when the pressure is uniform over

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:23.280
that area.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.660
Imagine two hundred newtons spread over zero point zero two square metres.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.060
Divide two hundred by zero point zero two, and you get ten thousand pascals.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.500
Now spread the same force over twice the area.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:37.880
What happens?

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:39.260
Give it a second.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:40.900
The pressure halves.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.520
Same force, more space to share it.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.420
A fluid at rest pushes perpendicular to the surfaces touching it.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.000
Pressure itself is a scalar, even though each pressure force has a direction.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.680
We'll also need density: mass divided by volume.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.100
Keep those two ideas separate.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.700
Density tells us how much mass occupies a volume; pressure tells us how

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.360
intensely a surface is being pushed.

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:07.868
Why do your ears notice when you go deeper underwater?

00:01:08.628 --> 00:01:11.208
There's more water above you, and the pressure increases.

00:01:12.108 --> 00:01:14.348
Picture a stationary vertical column of liquid.

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:19.088
The force from below must balance the force from above plus the column's

00:01:19.088 --> 00:01:19.288
weight.

00:01:20.288 --> 00:01:23.928
Divide that balance by the column's area, and the pressure increase becomes

00:01:23.928 --> 00:01:26.408
density times g times vertical depth.

00:01:27.048 --> 00:01:29.168
That's rho g h.

00:01:29.168 --> 00:01:33.388
At two metres below the surface of water, use density one thousand kilograms

00:01:33.388 --> 00:01:35.828
per cubic metre and g equal to ten.

00:01:36.728 --> 00:01:40.308
Multiply one thousand by ten by two: twenty thousand pascals.

00:01:41.148 --> 00:01:45.048
That's the pressure above the surface pressure, called gauge pressure when the

00:01:45.048 --> 00:01:46.208
surface is open to the atmosphere.

00:01:47.288 --> 00:01:51.688
If atmospheric pressure is given as one hundred thousand pascals, the absolute

00:01:51.688 --> 00:01:54.268
pressure is one hundred twenty thousand.

00:01:54.268 --> 00:01:56.468
A small but important extra step.

00:01:57.228 --> 00:01:59.968
Use vertical depth, not the distance along a sloping wall.

00:02:00.488 --> 00:02:04.588
And in the same connected stationary liquid of constant density, points at the

00:02:04.588 --> 00:02:06.008
same level have equal pressure.

00:02:06.768 --> 00:02:08.768
The container's shape doesn't get a vote.

00:02:09.024 --> 00:02:12.624
Now for the neat connection: pressure explains buoyancy.

00:02:13.944 --> 00:02:16.324
Imagine a block completely surrounded by still water.

00:02:17.004 --> 00:02:21.124
Its bottom is deeper than its top, so the upward pressure force on the bottom

00:02:21.124 --> 00:02:23.504
is larger than the downward force on the top.

00:02:24.184 --> 00:02:26.584
The side forces cancel horizontally.

00:02:27.364 --> 00:02:29.484
What's left is an upward resultant.

00:02:30.404 --> 00:02:34.364
Archimedes' principle says this buoyant force equals the weight of the fluid

00:02:34.364 --> 00:02:34.884
displaced.

00:02:35.544 --> 00:02:36.604
Not the weight of the object.

00:02:37.364 --> 00:02:38.724
That's the detail worth circling.

00:02:40.084 --> 00:02:43.824
Write buoyant force as fluid density times g times displaced volume.

00:02:44.684 --> 00:02:49.084
If the object displaces zero point zero zero two cubic metres of water,

00:02:49.904 --> 00:02:53.224
multiply one thousand by ten by zero point zero zero two.

00:02:53.224 --> 00:02:55.104
You get twenty newtons upward.

00:02:55.464 --> 00:03:00.084
If the object is partly submerged, use only its submerged volume, not its

00:03:00.084 --> 00:03:00.704
entire volume.

00:03:01.424 --> 00:03:05.464
We're assuming the fluid surrounds the wetted surfaces and the object isn't

00:03:05.464 --> 00:03:06.464
sealed against the bottom.

00:03:07.344 --> 00:03:11.644
For a rigid, fully submerged object in constant density water, going deeper

00:03:11.644 --> 00:03:13.632
doesn't by itself increase buoyancy: the displaced volume stays the same.

00:03:13.632 --> 00:03:18.732
Let's float a uniform block whose density is six hundred kilograms per cubic

00:03:18.732 --> 00:03:21.812
metre in water of density one thousand.

00:03:21.812 --> 00:03:23.552
How much of it sits below the surface?

00:03:24.412 --> 00:03:26.032
Resist the urge to guess from the picture.

00:03:26.892 --> 00:03:27.612
Start with forces.

00:03:28.332 --> 00:03:32.752
For a freely floating block at rest, with no other vertical forces, buoyancy

00:03:32.752 --> 00:03:33.572
equals weight.

00:03:34.272 --> 00:03:38.972
Fluid density times submerged volume times g equals body density times total

00:03:38.972 --> 00:03:40.032
volume times g.

00:03:40.812 --> 00:03:42.072
Cancel g and rearrange.

00:03:42.072 --> 00:03:47.232
Submerged volume divided by total volume equals body density divided by fluid

00:03:47.232 --> 00:03:47.612
density.

00:03:47.612 --> 00:03:51.692
Six hundred divided by one thousand is zero point six.

00:03:51.972 --> 00:03:55.752
So sixty percent is submerged and forty percent is above water.

00:03:56.532 --> 00:03:58.572
Nice: we didn't need the block's size.

00:03:59.132 --> 00:04:01.192
We're neglecting air buoyancy and surface tension.

00:04:01.812 --> 00:04:05.672
If you add a load, the block must displace more water to support the extra

00:04:05.672 --> 00:04:05.912
weight.

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:09.992
It settles lower until the forces balance, provided it can still float.

00:04:09.992 --> 00:04:12.652
A body's average density matters here.

00:04:13.192 --> 00:04:17.052
A hollow object can have a much lower average density than the material making

00:04:17.052 --> 00:04:17.552
its walls.

00:04:18.192 --> 00:04:20.192
Let's finish with a spring balance.

00:04:20.932 --> 00:04:25.612
A three kilogram object hangs at rest and is fully immersed in water without

00:04:25.612 --> 00:04:26.492
touching the container.

00:04:27.492 --> 00:04:30.672
Its volume is zero point zero zero two cubic metres.

00:04:31.792 --> 00:04:35.712
Use g equal to ten and water density one thousand.

00:04:35.712 --> 00:04:36.872
What does the balance read?

00:04:37.732 --> 00:04:40.512
First, the gravitational weight is thirty newtons.

00:04:41.152 --> 00:04:45.292
Next, buoyancy is twenty newtons, using the displaced volume.

00:04:46.172 --> 00:04:51.032
Now draw the forces: tension upward, buoyancy upward, and weight downward.

00:04:52.112 --> 00:04:55.812
Since the object is at rest, tension plus buoyancy equals weight.

00:04:56.492 --> 00:04:59.352
The tension is ten newtons, and that's the balance reading.

00:05:00.052 --> 00:05:01.652
The object's mass hasn't changed.

00:05:02.672 --> 00:05:04.552
Water is providing part of the upward support.

00:05:04.552 --> 00:05:10.212
Here's your check: if someone reports minus ten newtons of tension, revisit the

00:05:10.212 --> 00:05:10.792
assumed equilibrium.

00:05:11.832 --> 00:05:15.772
An ordinary string cannot push downward to keep a buoyant object submerged.

00:05:16.572 --> 00:05:22.152
Before each fluid statics problem, ask: absolute or gauge pressure, total or

00:05:22.152 --> 00:05:24.612
submerged volume, and which forces actually act?

00:05:25.212 --> 00:05:28.392
Get those choices right, and the equations become much friendlier.

00:05:29.352 --> 00:05:33.252
Flowing fluids are a different chapter; today's results describe fluids at

00:05:33.252 --> 00:05:33.552
rest.
